          </table></div>
          <p>
            Agreement on the core figures is unusually complete: the dimensions, weight and capacity are
            identical in Bowflex’s 2026 page, its 2020 manual and reviews from 2024 and 2025, which suggests
            the machine was not revised in any way that matters while it was on sale. That helps a used
            buyer, because the label on a Treadmill 10 should match these numbers whichever year it was made.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="on-the-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Deck, Decline, Motor and What Reviewers Reported</h2>
<p>
            The Treadmill 10’s running area is 22 by 60 inches, the same as Bowflex’s current T9 and T16 and
            two inches wider than most folding treadmills near its old price. Sixty inches of length suits
            adult runners, and the extra width helps tall and broad users and anyone whose stride wanders as
            they tire.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The decline is the rare part</h3>
          <p>
            The deck runs from -5% to 15% on a motorised lift. Incline to 15% is common in Bowflex’s range;
            the decline is not. None of the T6, T9 or T16 offers any, so a Treadmill 10 is now one of the few
            ways to get decline in a Bowflex at all. Decline lets you rehearse downhill running, which loads
            the quadriceps eccentrically and is useful before a hilly race; it is less useful for general
            fitness, and it loads the knees and thighs differently from flat running, so build into it gradually; if you have
            knee problems, check with a clinician before you add decline work. If decline is the reason you are
            looking, compare the new machines in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with decline</a>
            roundup before settling on a used one. Bowflex’s manual notes that the deck ships in a declined
            position and must be set to 0 to stand level, and that it should be set to -5 before you move
            or fold the machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 400 lb rating</h3>
          <p>
            A 400 lb maximum user weight, printed in both the manual and the product page, is higher than any
            treadmill Bowflex sells today: the T6 is rated to 325 lb, the T9 to 350 lb and the T16 to
            375 lb. For a heavier user that margin is a real reason to consider a Treadmill 10. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains why a rating well above your weight matters for the motor and deck over years of use.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No published motor rating</h3>
          <p>
            Bowflex never published a horsepower figure for the Treadmill 10. Two reviewers say they asked
            and were not given one; figures of 4.0 HP that appear in some listings are not confirmed by
            Bowflex, and we do not repeat them as fact. The useful signal is the warranty: the owner’s manual
            gives 15 years on the drive motor, though that cover belonged to the first owner. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why a motor rating, when it exists, is less informative than its basis and its warranty.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What more than one reviewer reported</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It is very large and heavy.</strong> All three reviews make this the main drawback; Tom’s Guide suggests planning a 4 by 8 foot space.</li>
            <li><strong>Assembly is long and fiddly.</strong> Tom’s Guide found bolt holes that did not line up, one bolt that never threaded, and about two and a half hours from unboxing to finished; Treadmill Review Guru recommends two or three people; BarBend says you need an extra pair of hands.</li>
            <li><strong>Moving it is a struggle.</strong> Two reviews note you have to tip the whole machine back onto its wheels.</li>
            <li><strong>The screen is small for streaming.</strong> Tom’s Guide and BarBend both say so.</li>
            <li><strong>Content leaned on JRNY.</strong> All three reviews, written in 2024 and 2025, say streaming and most guided content needed a paid JRNY membership. Bowflex has since changed that, as the next section explains.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The reviewers disagree about the cushioning, so we draw no conclusion from it. Points made by only one
            reviewer, such as occasional static shocks on carpet without a mat, sticky side-handle buttons and
            a tinny speaker, are worth knowing but are not patterns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-jrny" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Console, JRNY, and What Works Without a Subscription</h2>
<p>
            The Treadmill 10 is built around a 10-inch HD touchscreen running Bowflex’s JRNY software. Speed
            and incline can also be changed from buttons on the side and upper handlebars, and Bowflex’s
            manual describes preset speed and incline buttons, Bluetooth audio and a Bluetooth heart-rate
            receiver for the included armband. Tom’s Guide also describes a built-in fan and speaker.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wi-Fi registration comes first</h3>
          <p>
            This is the point a used buyer most needs to know. The owner’s manual says a Wi-Fi connection is
            required to set the machine up: on first start the console asks you to connect, may download a
            software update, and then asks you to log in to or create a JRNY account to register the machine.
            After that initial registration, the manual says, the treadmill can be run without Wi-Fi, but only
            the basic workouts and functions are available; coaching, custom workouts, Explore the World
            routes and trainer-led videos need Wi-Fi and a JRNY membership.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">JRNY terms then and now</h3>
          <p>
            When the Treadmill 10 was on sale, the reviews describe a fairly locked-down arrangement: manual
            mode and a set of standard workouts worked without a membership, while streaming apps and most
            guided content needed one. Tom’s Guide and BarBend give a two-month free trial, then $19.99 a month
            or $149 a year; Treadmill Review Guru, in 2024, describes the first year as free. Those were
            probably different promotions at different times.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bowflex’s terms are looser now. On 20 October 2026 its US JRNY page said a paid membership is not
            required to use any Bowflex equipment, that a free JRNY account gives access to a selection of
            trainer-led classes, and that on machines with a built-in screen a free account can stream
            entertainment apps using your own subscriptions to them. The paid tiers were JRNY All-Access at
            $19.99 a month or $149 a year, which Bowflex describes as required for the full experience on most
            of its touchscreen machines, and the JRNY App at $11.99 a month or $99 a year for use on a phone or
            tablet. Bowflex’s discontinued Treadmill 10 page carries the same free-account wording, with the
            footnote that a membership is required for the full JRNY experience.
          </p>
          <p>
            What we cannot confirm is how long Bowflex will keep updating the software on a discontinued
            console. Before you buy a used one, see the JRNY home screen working on that machine, signed in to
            an account, and confirm the previous owner has removed the machine from theirs. If a subscription
            is what you want to avoid, our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            covers machines that need no account at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Warranty, the 2024 Ownership Change and Recalls</h2>
<p>
            Bowflex’s owner’s manual for the Treadmill 10, issued by Nautilus, Inc., gives these US and Canada
            terms: 15 years on the frame, 15 years on the drive motor, 5 years on mechanical parts, 1 year on
            electronics and 2 years of labour. Labour does not cover fitting parts that are part of the initial
            assembly, and repairs under it had to be authorised in advance. Bowflex’s 2026 product page gives the
            same frame, parts, electronics and labour terms but does not list the motor, and Tom’s Guide
            describes the frame cover as “frame-only”. The manual is the formal warranty document, so we take it
            as the stronger source while noting the conflict.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The clause that matters for a used buyer</h3>
          <p>
            The manual states that the warranty “is valid only to the original purchaser and is not
            transferable or applicable to any other person(s)”. It also excludes machines used commercially or
            for more than three hours a day, and damage from exceeding the user weight. So whatever years a
            seller tells you are left, a second owner should assume they have no warranty at all and price the
            machine accordingly. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how frame, motor, parts and labour terms interact.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What changed after Johnson Health Tech bought Bowflex</h3>
          <p>
            BowFlex Inc., the former Nautilus, Inc., filed for Chapter 11 in March 2024, and Johnson Health Tech,
            which also owns Horizon and Matrix, bought the BowFlex, Schwinn and JRNY brands in a sale that
            closed in April 2024. Two things follow for Treadmill 10 owners.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The current range carries different terms.</strong> Bowflex’s T6, T9 and T16 come with a lifetime warranty on the frame and motor, 3 years on parts and 1 year of labour. Against the Treadmill 10’s terms, that is longer cover on the frame and motor, but two years less on parts and a year less of labour.</li>
            <li><strong>Pre-sale warranties are uncertain.</strong> We found no published statement from Johnson Health Tech on whether it honours warranties on machines sold by Nautilus or BowFlex Inc. before the sale. The one official signal we found points the other way: in the 2025 recall of BowFlex adjustable dumbbells, the CPSC notice offers a voucher or a replacement for units sold by Johnson Health Tech, but only a prorated voucher plus a year of JRNY for units sold by BowFlex Inc. If you own a Treadmill 10 bought before April 2024, ask Bowflex about your cover in writing, quoting the serial number.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Bowflex kept selling the Treadmill 10 after the sale, as the February 2025 Tom’s Guide review shows,
            so some units were bought new from Johnson Health Tech. If yours was, keep that receipt; it is what
            separates a Johnson Health Tech warranty from a Nautilus one. Our
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand guide</a>
            covers the ownership change in more detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the CPSC recall database for Bowflex and Nautilus on 20 October 2026 and found no recall
            of the Treadmill 10 or the Treadmill 22. Bowflex’s own recalls page lists only the 552 and 1090
            adjustable dumbbells. The one Nautilus treadmill recall, in June 2022, covered Nautilus-branded T616
            and T618 treadmills in specific serial ranges, which could start on their own; it does not include
            the Treadmill 10. Johnson Health Tech’s 2024 and 2026 treadmill recalls cover Matrix and Vision
            machines, not Bowflex. Check cpsc.gov again before buying, since recalls can be issued at any time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-moving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Footprint, Folding and Ceiling Height</h2>
<p>
            Assembled, the Treadmill 10 is 85 inches long, 39.6 inches wide and 65.3 inches tall, which is about
            23.4 square feet of floor. It folds with Bowflex’s SoftDrop system, which lowers the deck slowly, to
            44.5 inches long but 70 inches tall, so it stands taller folded than in use. At 323 lb it is about
            40 lb heavier than the T9.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bowflex’s manual asks for a minimum workout area of 79 by 164 inches, which includes room behind the
            belt, on a hard, level surface. Compare that with the room you have before you agree to collect one,
            and plan the route in: a 323 lb machine needs at least two people, and two reviewers note that you
            have to tip it back onto its wheels. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space guide</a>
            covers clearances and doorways.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ceiling height: two figures that do not quite agree</h3>
          <p>
            Bowflex’s product page gives a minimum ceiling height of the user’s height plus 12.4 inches. Its manual
            gives the deck’s maximum inclined height as 18.2 inches and tells you to allow for the user’s height and
            the maximum incline. Those two figures are hard to square, since the deck at full incline already sits
            higher than 12.4 inches. We would plan on the more cautious reading: stand on the belt with the deck at
            15% and check you have comfortable headroom before you run. Bowflex gives the T9, a similar machine,
            the user’s height plus 20.75 inches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Buying a Used Bowflex Treadmill 10</h2>
<p>
            A used Treadmill 10 is a reasonable buy only if the price reflects three facts: no warranty passes to
            you, no motor rating was ever published, and a new Bowflex with a lifetime frame and motor warranty
            starts at $999 on sale. Our general
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            applies; these are the checks specific to this machine.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Confirm the model.</strong> Read the label on the machine. The Treadmill 22 has the same deck size but a 22-inch screen and a range of -5% to 20%, and older Bowflex BXT models have different figures. Bowflex lists the Treadmill 10 under SKU HTM1480-OS; note the model and serial number from the machine’s own label.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it through the whole range.</strong> Walk and jog on it, take it to 15% and down to -5%, and listen to the incline motor at both ends. Decline is the feature you are paying for and the one that is easiest to leave untested.</li>
            <li><strong>Boot the console.</strong> Watch it start, connect to Wi-Fi and reach the JRNY home screen. A touchscreen that will not register or update is the costliest fault on this machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Get the account released.</strong> Ask the seller to remove the machine from their JRNY account before you take it, so it can be registered to you.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the deck and belt.</strong> Look for belt fraying, deck wear and signs it has never been lubricated; Bowflex sells a Lube-N-Walk kit for its treadmills.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask for the receipt.</strong> It tells you whether it was sold by Nautilus or Johnson Health Tech and when, which is what you will need for any conversation with Bowflex about parts.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to pay</h3>
          <p>
            We found no reliable record of used sale prices, so we will not invent a number. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used guide</a>
            gives a rule of thumb: roughly half the original price at two years and about a third at three to five.
            Applied to the $1,999 US list price the reviews report, that suggests about $1,000 for a two-year-old
            machine and roughly $650 to $700 for an older one in good order. Treat those as ceilings, not targets:
            a new T6 at $999 or a T9 at $1,499 on sale comes with a lifetime frame and motor warranty in your name,
            and is delivered rather than collected.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where you look matters too. Our guide to
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a>
            compares local listings, resellers and refurbishers. Bowflex runs a replacement-parts site, parts.bowflex.com,
            but we could not confirm which Treadmill 10 parts it stocks, so ask before you buy if a specific part looks worn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-current-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
