              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">323 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">336 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">282 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">288 or 293.2 lb (Bowflex gives both)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame / motor warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 yr / 15 yr, first owner</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 yr frame (Bowflex page)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime / lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime / lifetime</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Parts / labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 yr / 2 yr</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 yr / 2 yr</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 yr / 1 yr</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 yr / 1 yr</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price, 20 Oct 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Used only ($1,999 list when sold)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Used only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,799 regular, $1,499 on sale</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,299 regular, $1,999 on sale</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Treadmill 10 or a new T9</h3>
          <p>
            The deck and top speed are the same, and the T9 adds a lifetime frame and motor warranty in your own
            name, a published motor rating (3.5 HP, which Bowflex footnotes as a test-setting figure), a console
            that works fully without any subscription, and a smaller, lighter frame. The Treadmill 10 keeps two advantages: decline to
            -5% and a 400 lb rating against 350 lb. If you need neither, the T9 is the better buy at anything close
            to its sale price.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Treadmill 10 or a new T16</h3>
          <p>
            If what you liked about the Treadmill 10 was the built-in screen, the T16’s 16-inch touchscreen is
            larger, and Tom’s Guide, whose reviewer used both, wrote that the T16 “outpaces the T10 in all categories”,
            with complaints limited to the speakers, a lower-profile stop button and the deck release. It is rated
            to 375 lb and has no decline.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If you need decline or more than 375 lb</h3>
          <p>
            No current Bowflex does both. A used Treadmill 10 or Treadmill 22 is one route; a new machine from
            another brand with decline, a high weight rating and a current warranty is the other. The
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a>
            lists machines we could confirm on sale with decline, and the
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6 review</a>
            covers the cheapest current Bowflex if your needs are simpler than either.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy a Bowflex Treadmill 10 Now</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a used one if you want decline in a big, well-specified machine and the price is right.</strong>
            A -5% to 15% range on a 22 by 60-inch deck is not something Bowflex sells new any more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider one if you need a 400 lb rating.</strong> That is 25 lb more than the T16 and 50 lb more
            than the T9. Weigh it against the lack of any warranty, and run it hard before paying.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you mainly want a Bowflex for walking, incline walking or running at average build.</strong>
            The T6 or T9 does that new, with a lifetime frame and motor warranty, for prices that overlap with
            what a seller will want for a Treadmill 10.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you want a screen and no hassle.</strong> The T16 has a larger touchscreen, a warranty in
            your name and, by the account of a reviewer who used both, is better to live with.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already own one,</strong> find your receipt, note the serial number, and ask Bowflex in
            writing what cover it will honour, especially if you bought it before April 2024.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bowflex Treadmill 10 discontinued?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. On 20 October 2026 Bowflex’s US store no longer listed it, its old product page redirected to the current treadmill range, and Bowflex’s Canadian store labelled it discontinued and suggested the T9. Tom’s Guide described it as discontinued in December 2025.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What replaced the Bowflex Treadmill 10?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bowflex points Treadmill 10 shoppers to the T9, which has the same 22 by 60-inch deck. Tom’s Guide calls the T16 its successor, since the T16 is now Bowflex’s touchscreen treadmill. Neither has decline, and neither is rated to 400 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the Bowflex Treadmill 10 specs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 22 by 60-inch running area, 0 to 12 mph, a motorised range from -5% decline to 15% incline, a 400 lb maximum user weight, a 10-inch HD touchscreen and a machine weight of 323 lb. It measured 85 by 39.6 by 65.3 inches and folded to 44.5 inches long. Bowflex never published a motor rating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Bowflex Treadmill 10 need a JRNY subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to run. Its manual says it must be registered over Wi-Fi with a JRNY account, after which basic workouts work offline. Bowflex now says a free account can stream apps using your own subscriptions; full JRNY coaching needs All-Access, $19.99 a month or $149 a year on 20 October 2026.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty did the Bowflex Treadmill 10 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Its owner’s manual gives 15 years on the frame and drive motor, 5 years on mechanical parts, 1 year on electronics and 2 years of labour in the US and Canada, valid only for the original purchaser. A used buyer should assume no warranty transfers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a used Bowflex Treadmill 10 worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is cheap, you have tested it through its full incline and decline range, and you want decline or a 400 lb rating. Otherwise a new T9, $1,499 on sale at bowflex.com on 20 October 2026, has the same deck size and a lifetime frame and motor warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Bowflex Treadmill 10</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A discontinued machine with a strong specification invites a few predictable errors. These are the ones that cost used buyers money.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the remaining warranty comes with it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bowflex’s manual says the Treadmill 10 warranty is valid only for the original purchaser and is not transferable. A seller saying there are “years left on the motor” is describing cover you will not get. Price the machine as having no warranty.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing only on the flat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Decline to -5% is the feature that sets the Treadmill 10 apart, and the easiest to leave untried. Run it at 15% and at -5% before paying, and listen to the incline motor at both ends of its travel.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a console you cannot sign in to</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The machine has to be registered over Wi-Fi with a JRNY account. Watch it boot, connect and reach the home screen, and ask the seller to remove it from their account so it can be registered to you.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying close to the price of a new T9</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A new T9 has the same 22 by 60-inch deck and a lifetime frame and motor warranty, and was $1,499 on sale on 20 October 2026. Unless you need decline or the 400 lb rating, a used Treadmill 10 near that price is the wrong buy.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

